// Reference query. Not deployed in any employer environment.
// Title: q02, sign-ins from legacy authentication clients, including authenticated SMTP
// Status: untested template
// ATT&CK: T1078.004 (Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts); T1110.003 (Brute Force: Password Spraying). MITRE ATT&CK v20
// Data sources: EntraIdSignInEvents in Microsoft Defender XDR advanced hunting (preview, as of 2026-10-13), which holds Microsoft Entra interactive and non-interactive user sign-ins
// Licensing assumptions: Microsoft states that this table needs Microsoft Entra ID P2, which Fabrikam (fictional) gets with Microsoft Defender Suite on Microsoft 365 E3 (ADR-001); Microsoft 365 E5 also includes it. On E3 alone, filter the Microsoft Entra sign-in logs by Client App, as Microsoft's own steps describe.
//
// Purpose
//   Legacy protocols (POP, IMAP, authenticated SMTP, Exchange ActiveSync, and older clients)
//   cannot do multifactor authentication. Microsoft recommends blocking them and says that, by
//   its own analysis, more than 99 percent of password spray attacks use legacy authentication
//   protocols. Successful sign-ins are the dependencies to migrate before the legacy
//   authentication block in document 06 (action 06.10) and before Exchange Online turns
//   SMTP AUTH Basic authentication off by default for existing tenants at the end of
//   December 2026 (actions 06.05 and 06.15; the date comes from Microsoft's message center as
//   relayed in document 06). Failed sign-ins from these clients are often password guessing.
//
// How to read the result
//   Microsoft's guidance: a client shown as browser or as a modern mobile or desktop client uses
//   modern authentication, while a specific protocol name, such as Exchange ActiveSync, means
//   legacy authentication. The query applies that rule and returns one row per account, client,
//   and outcome.
//   Outcome "success": the migration list. Give each row an owner and a fix (OAuth 2.0, the
//   Microsoft Graph sendMail API, or retirement). SmtpAuth marks the scanners and applications
//   that send mail this way.
//   Outcome "failure" from many addresses against many accounts: likely password spraying.
//   Hand it to the on-call (document 08).
//   Once the block is enforced, expect failures only.
//
// Which KPI it feeds
//   No catalog KPI directly. It is the work list for document 06 actions 06.05, 06.10, and
//   06.15, and it supports KPI-08 by closing sign-in paths that skip MFA.
//
// Tuning
//   LookBack: at most 30 days. Accept a remaining legacy sender only as a documented exception
//   with an owner and an end date.
//
// References (checked 2026-10-13)
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/policy-block-legacy-authentication
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/monitoring-health/workbook-legacy-authentication
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/advanced-hunting-entraidsigninevents-table
let LookBack = 30d;
EntraIdSignInEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(LookBack)
| where isnotempty(ClientAppUsed)
| extend ModernClient = ClientAppUsed has "Browser" or (ClientAppUsed has "Mobile" and ClientAppUsed has "Desktop")
| where not(ModernClient)
| extend Outcome = iff(ErrorCode == 0, "success", "failure")
| summarize
    SignIns = count(),
    FirstSeen = min(Timestamp),
    LastSeen = max(Timestamp),
    DistinctIPs = dcount(IPAddress),
    IPAddresses = make_set(IPAddress, 20),
    Countries = make_set(Country, 10),
    Applications = make_set(Application, 10)
    by AccountUpn, ClientAppUsed, Outcome
| extend SmtpAuth = ClientAppUsed has "SMTP"
| order by Outcome desc, SignIns desc
